1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,285
A dozen means twelve eggs.

2
00:00:02,285 --> 00:00:05,091
A ream means five hundred sheets of paper.

3
00:00:05,091 --> 00:00:07,531
Counting words make big numbers easy.

4
00:00:07,931 --> 00:00:12,402
But atoms are so tiny that one sip
of water holds an unthinkable number

5
00:00:12,402 --> 00:00:13,302
of them.

6
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:16,918
Aarav needs tiny steel
balls for his bicycle wheel.

7
00:00:16,918 --> 00:00:18,947
The shop does not count them.

8
00:00:18,947 --> 00:00:20,110
It weighs them.

9
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,684
Chemists count atoms the same way.

10
00:00:22,684 --> 00:00:23,617
By weighing.

11
00:00:24,117 --> 00:00:27,594
By the end, you will count the
molecules in a sip of water.

12
00:00:28,244 --> 00:00:29,688
Meet the chemist's dozen.

13
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:30,588
The mole.

14
00:00:30,738 --> 00:00:35,448
One mole is the amount of a substance
that has as many particles as there

15
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:38,867
are atoms in exactly twelve
grams of carbon twelve.

16
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:42,211
Particles can be atoms, molecules or ions.

17
00:00:42,211 --> 00:00:44,909
The mole counts them all the same way.

18
00:00:45,509 --> 00:00:48,394
Let's count by weighing
on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:48,394 --> 00:00:50,978
Pour carbon twelve atoms onto the scale.

20
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:56,495
One carbon twelve atom has a mass of
one point nine nine times ten to the

21
00:00:56,495 --> 00:00:58,424
minus twenty three grams.

22
00:00:58,824 --> 00:01:01,902
Keep pouring until the
scale reads twelve grams.

23
00:01:01,902 --> 00:01:03,515
Watch the atom counter.

24
00:01:03,915 --> 00:01:08,760
It stops at six point zero two two
times ten to the twenty three atoms.

25
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:13,389
If you counted one atom every second,
it would take you far longer than

26
00:01:13,389 --> 00:01:15,019
the age of the universe.

27
00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:18,524
Here is where that number
comes from, step by step.

28
00:01:18,874 --> 00:01:19,778
Step one.

29
00:01:19,778 --> 00:01:25,023
One carbon twelve atom has a mass of
one point nine nine two six four eight

30
00:01:25,023 --> 00:01:28,142
times ten to the minus twenty three grams.

31
00:01:28,892 --> 00:01:29,792
Step two.

32
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:33,065
Divide twelve grams by
the mass of one atom.

33
00:01:33,815 --> 00:01:34,729
Step three.

34
00:01:34,729 --> 00:01:39,137
The answer is six point zero two
two times ten to the twenty three.

35
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:42,354
This is the Avogadro
constant, NA, per mole.

36
00:01:43,104 --> 00:01:44,067
Step four.

37
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:46,425
One u times NA is one gram.

38
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:49,991
So a particle of x u
makes a mole of x grams.

39
00:01:50,741 --> 00:01:55,849
Now compare one mole of hydrogen atoms
with one mole of water molecules.

40
00:01:56,249 --> 00:02:00,249
The hydrogen atoms weigh one
point zero zero eight grams.

41
00:02:00,249 --> 00:02:03,579
The water weighs eighteen
point zero two grams.

42
00:02:03,979 --> 00:02:05,184
Pause and think.

43
00:02:05,184 --> 00:02:07,192
Which mole has more particles?

44
00:02:10,392 --> 00:02:11,292
Neither!

45
00:02:11,144 --> 00:02:16,161
Both hold six point zero two two times
ten to the twenty three particles.

46
00:02:16,161 --> 00:02:18,934
Only the mass of each particle differs.

47
00:02:19,584 --> 00:02:23,287
The mass of one mole, in grams,
is called the molar mass.

48
00:02:23,287 --> 00:02:25,093
Its unit is grams per mole.

49
00:02:25,493 --> 00:02:29,937
Water has a molar mass of eighteen
point zero two grams per mole.

50
00:02:29,937 --> 00:02:33,716
Sodium chloride, fifty eight
point five grams per mole.

51
00:02:34,366 --> 00:02:35,352
Remember this.

52
00:02:35,352 --> 00:02:37,500
A mole is a count, not a mass.

53
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:39,936
Now, one worked example.

54
00:02:40,236 --> 00:02:43,019
Aarav takes a sip of water, nine grams.

55
00:02:43,019 --> 00:02:46,734
Take the molar mass of water
as eighteen grams per mole.

56
00:02:47,234 --> 00:02:48,190
Step one.

57
00:02:48,190 --> 00:02:51,303
Moles equals mass divided by molar mass.

58
00:02:51,303 --> 00:02:55,081
Nine divided by eighteen
is zero point five mole.

59
00:02:55,831 --> 00:02:56,760
Step two.

60
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,320
Molecules equals moles times NA.

61
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:04,871
Zero point five times six point zero
two two times ten to the twenty three.

62
00:03:05,621 --> 00:03:10,757
That is three point zero one one times
ten to the twenty three water molecules,

63
00:03:10,757 --> 00:03:12,134
in one small sip.

64
00:03:12,784 --> 00:03:14,250
Now, the exam trap.

65
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:19,310
Asked for the atoms in nine grams of
water, students stop at three point

66
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:22,327
zero one one times ten
to the twenty three.

67
00:03:22,727 --> 00:03:24,612
But that counts molecules.

68
00:03:24,612 --> 00:03:27,149
Each water molecule has three atoms.

69
00:03:27,149 --> 00:03:28,771
So multiply by three.

70
00:03:28,771 --> 00:03:33,162
Nine point zero three three times
ten to the twenty three atoms.

71
00:03:33,812 --> 00:03:35,273
Where is this asked?

72
00:03:35,273 --> 00:03:37,029
In nearly every numerical.

73
00:03:37,029 --> 00:03:40,148
Grams to moles, moles
to particles, and back.

74
00:03:40,748 --> 00:03:42,025
Exam level now.

75
00:03:42,025 --> 00:03:46,765
How many formula units are in one
hundred and seventeen grams of sodium

76
00:03:46,765 --> 00:03:47,665
chloride?

77
00:03:47,587 --> 00:03:48,918
Pause and solve.

78
00:03:54,318 --> 00:03:55,699
The answer is B.

79
00:03:56,199 --> 00:04:01,097
Moles equals one hundred and seventeen
divided by fifty eight point five.

80
00:04:01,097 --> 00:04:02,570
That is two moles.

81
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:08,224
Two times six point zero two two times
ten to the twenty three is one point

82
00:04:08,224 --> 00:04:11,742
two zero four four times
ten to the twenty four.

83
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:14,366
C counts every ion separately.

84
00:04:14,366 --> 00:04:17,184
But the question asked for formula units.

85
00:04:17,784 --> 00:04:19,245
Here is your recap.

86
00:04:19,245 --> 00:04:20,747
Screenshot this card.

87
00:04:23,947 --> 00:04:26,783
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

88
00:04:27,183 --> 00:04:28,083
One.

89
00:04:27,658 --> 00:04:30,109
What is one mole, in your own words?

90
00:04:30,909 --> 00:04:31,809
Two.

91
00:04:31,422 --> 00:04:35,839
Why does a mole of water weigh
more than a mole of hydrogen atoms?

92
00:04:36,639 --> 00:04:37,539
Three.

93
00:04:37,306 --> 00:04:41,421
How many moles are in forty
four grams of carbon dioxide?

94
00:04:42,321 --> 00:04:46,088
Next time: how much of
water's mass is really oxygen?

95
00:04:46,088 --> 00:04:48,752
We find out in percentage composition.
